  "source_solutions": [
    {
      "number": "665",
      "task": "Read four forward values and four inverse values from the graph.",
      "solution": "For a), read the y coordinate at each given x: (0.1,0.25,0.72,0.865) gives approximately (1.3,1.8,5.2,7.3). Internal evaluation checks give (1.258925,1.778279,5.248075,7.328245). For b), read the x coordinate where the curve meets each y: (3,5,6.2,9.37) gives approximately (0.48,0.70,0.79,0.97). Exact inverse descriptions are log10(3), log10(5), log10(6.2), log10(9.37). Forward and inverse readings use opposite projection directions.",
      "source_quantity_count": 8
    },
    {
      "number": "666",
      "task": "Read five forward exponential values within the displayed domain.",
      "solution": "At x=(0.2,0.6,0.47,0.83,0.584), read approximately y=(1.6,4.0,3.0,6.8,3.8). Internal evaluation checks are (1.584893,3.981072,2.951209,6.760830,3.837072). Values near .584 and .6 require interpolation on the same increasing curve, not a linear y=10x rule.",
      "source_quantity_count": 5
    },
    {
      "number": "667",
      "task": "Read a shared fractional part and extend to positive and negative integer shifts.",
      "solution": "Let A=10^.4, visually about 2.5; the a) values are exactly (A,10A,A/10,A/1000), approximately (2.5,25,.25,.0025). Let B=10^.59, visually about 3.9; the b) values are exactly (B,1000B,B/10,B/10000), approximately (3.9,3900,.39,.00039). For negative exponents use -.6=-1+.4, -2.6=-3+.4, -.41=-1+.59, -3.41=-4+.59. Internal checks: A=2.5118864315 and B=3.8904514499.",
      "source_quantity_count": 8
    },
    {
      "number": "668",
      "task": "Decompose each exponent into an integer plus a fractional part in [0,1] and scale a graph reading.",
      "solution": "1.38=1+.38 gives 10*10^.38, about 24; 2.61=2+.61 gives 100*10^.61, about 410; -.44=-1+.56 gives .1*10^.56, about .36; -1.25=-2+.75 gives .01*10^.75, about .056; -3.06=-4+.94 gives .0001*10^.94, about .00087. Internal checks are (23.988329,407.380278,.363078,.0562341,.000870964). In particular the negative integer part is the floor, not truncation toward zero.",
      "source_quantity_count": 5
    },
    {
      "number": "669",
      "task": "Read four inverse exponents for given exponential values.",
      "solution": "Read x at y=(1.5,3.7,6.8,8.8): approximately (.18,.57,.83,.94). Exact inverse descriptions are log10 of the four outputs; internal checks are (.176091,.568202,.832509,.944483). The inverse is not y/10 or the inverse of a straight line between the graph endpoints.",
      "source_quantity_count": 4
    },
    {
      "number": "670",
      "task": "Represent all seventeen numbers as powers of 10, retaining decimal-shift relationships and the independent value 200.",
      "solution": "a) (1,10,10000,.1,.001)=10^(0,1,4,-1,-3). b) If b=log10(7), the exponents for (7,70,.7,.007) are (b,b+1,b-1,b-3), with b about .845098. c) If c=log10(3.8), the exponents for (3.8,380,.38,.038) are (c,c+2,c-1,c-2), with c about .579784. d) If d=log10(5.2), the exponents for (5.2,200,.52,.0052) are (d,2+log10(2),d-1,d-3), with d about .716003 and 2+log10(2) about 2.301030. The second d) value is 200, not 520; it is not a shift of 5.2.",
      "source_quantity_count": 17
    },
    {
      "number": "671",
      "task": "Convert all six radicals/products to base-10 powers.",
      "solution": "a) sqrt(10)=10^(1/2). b) cbrt(100)=10^(2/3). c) 10sqrt(10)=10^(1+1/2)=10^(3/2). d) .1sqrt(.1)=10^(-1-1/2)=10^(-3/2). e) .1cbrt(10)=10^(-1+1/3)=10^(-2/3). f) 100 fourthroot(10^-3)=10^(2-3/4)=10^(5/4). Root extraction divides the exponent by the root index, while multiplication of equal-base powers adds exponents.",
      "source_quantity_count": 6
    },
    {
      "number": "672",
      "task": "Solve six exponential equations, with linear and quadratic exponent equations distinguished.",
      "solution": "a) 10^(1-x^2)=10^0 implies x^2=1, so x=-1 or 1. b) sqrt(100^x)=sqrt(10^(2x))=10^x for every real x; 10^x=10^-1 implies x=-1. c) (10^-2)^x=10^3 implies -2x=3, so x=-3/2. d) 10^(3-2x)=10^(x^2) implies x^2+2x-3=(x+3)(x-1)=0, so x=-3 or 1. e) -2x=1/3 implies x=-1/6. f) 1000^x=10^(3x) and fifthroot(100)=10^(2/5); 3x=2/5 implies x=2/15. No real-domain exclusions arise from these positive bases.",
      "source_quantity_count": 6
    },
    {
      "number": "673",
      "task": "Solve six exponential inequalities with strict and inclusive endpoints preserved.",
      "solution": "a) 10^-x>10^1 implies -x>1, hence x<-1. b) x+1<4 gives x<3. c) x-2>-2 gives x>0. d) -1<=x<=4 gives the closed interval [-1,4]; both endpoints are included. e) 0<x+1<4 gives (-1,3). f) 10^-2<10^(2x)<10^(1/2) gives -2<2x<1/2, hence (-1,1/4). Dividing by a positive 2 does not reverse the comparison.",
      "source_quantity_count": 6
    },
    {
      "number": "674",
      "task": "Use a common base to solve two equations and two inequalities, including the mixed-number right side.",
      "solution": "a) 8=2^3 and 16=2^4 give 3(x-3)=4, hence x=13/3. b) 27=3^3 and 9=3^2 give 3(2x-1)=2, hence x=5/6. c) cbrt(144)=cbrt(12^2)=12^(2/3); since 12>1, x<2/3. d) Keep the source mixed number 15 5/8=125/8=(5/2)^3=(2/5)^-3 and .4=2/5. Since 0<.4<1, .4^x<.4^-3 gives x>-3. Equality at -3 is excluded.",
      "source_quantity_count": 4
    }
  ],
